  "slides": [
    {
      "id": "overview-cover",
      "title": "",
      "elements": [
        {
          "id": "text-1",
          "type": "text",
          "text": "APIOps Cycles"
        },
        {
          "id": "text-2",
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Connecting API strategy,\ndesign and delivery"
        },
        {
          "id": "text-3",
          "type": "text",
          "text": "A short introduction to APIOps Cycles"
        },
        {
          "id": "text-4",
          "type": "text",
          "text": "APIOps Cycles"
        },
        {
          "id": "image-5",
          "type": "image",
          "asset": "chaos-to-capabilities.png",
          "alt": "APIOps human scene showing connected discovery and delivery roles",
          "fit": "contain"
        }
      ],
      "notes": "Introduction to the APIOps Cycles method. The supplier-payment examples in this edition are fictional illustrations. Method and canvas attribution is preserved in the relevant slides."
    },
    {
      "id": "method-overview",
      "title": "APIOps Cycles connects decisions across the lifecycle",
      "elements": [
        {
          "id": "text-1",
          "type": "text",
          "text": "An open method for connecting consumer value with architecture, design, delivery and continuous improvement."
        },
        {
          "id": "table-2",
          "type": "table",
          "rows": [
            [
              "Cycle view",
              "Primary focus"
            ],
            [
              "Capability Productization",
              "Define a reusable capability and the service promise."
            ],
            [
              "API Productization",
              "Develop, publish and improve an API as a product."
            ],
            [
              "Integration Productization",
              "Connect providers and consumers through reusable integrations."
            ],
            [
              "Automation",
              "Design and govern workflows, agents and operational controls."
            ]
          ],
          "header": true,
          "emphasizeFirstColumn": true
        },
        {
          "id": "text-3",
          "type": "text",
          "text": "This introduction follows API Productization, with a financial-services case and an illustrative payment continuation."
        }
      ],
      "notes": "APIOps introduction. Technical continuation is illustrative; no client implementation or measured improvement is claimed.\n\nThe cycle catalog provides these four views over shared stations, resource guidance and readiness criteria. This deck follows the API Productization Cycle. Case-study statements derive from the supplied anonymised Word document. The supplier-payment canvases and subsequent technical decisions are illustrative.\n\nSources checked 3 November 2026:\nsrc/data/method/cycles.json\nsrc/lib/method-engine.js"
    },
    {
      "id": "productization-map",
      "title": "API Productization Cycle metro map",
      "elements": [
        {
          "id": "image-1",
          "type": "image",
          "asset": "api-productization-metro-neutral.png",
          "alt": "Official API Productization Cycle metro map exported from the repository application",
          "fit": "contain"
        },
        {
          "id": "text-2",
          "type": "text",
          "text": "One lifecycle connects the work"
        },
        {
          "id": "numbered-3",
          "type": "numbered",
          "items": [
            "API Product Strategy",
            "API Consumer Experience",
            "API Platform Architecture",
            "API Design",
            "API Delivery",
            "API Audit",
            "API Publishing",
            "API Monitoring & Improvement"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "text-4",
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The engagement connected discovery to design, delivery and testing across these stations."
        }
      ],
      "notes": "Source: Australian_Financial_Services_APIOps_Cycles_Case_Study (1).docx, supplied by the user. Client anonymised. Engagement by APIsFirst with partner Osaango. Benefits are qualitative; no quantified delivery or business impact is claimed.\n\nCanonical API Productization Cycle map exported through the local repository application at /cycles/api-productization-cycle/ using Export SVG. Public reference: https://apiopscycles.com/cycles/api-productization-cycle/ . Supporting lines cover business opportunities, platform architecture, design, delivery, publishing and adoption, and operating model. The map is a method overview, not evidence that every station was completed during the engagement."
    },
    {
      "id": "customer-journey",
      "title": "Customer Journey Canvas: a supplier payment",
      "elements": [
        {
          "id": "image-1",
          "type": "image",
          "asset": "filled-customerJourneyCanvas-neutral.png",
          "alt": "New illustrative filled Customer Journey Canvas for a small business owner paying a supplier invoice",
          "fit": "contain"
        },
        {
          "id": "text-2",
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Customer and trigger"
        },
        {
          "id": "text-3",
          "type": "text",
          "text": "A small business owner has a supplier bill due and wants to know the payment has completed."
        },
        {
          "id": "text-4",
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The customer journey"
        },
        {
          "id": "numbered-5",
          "type": "numbered",
          "items": [
            "Open the due bill",
            "Choose the payee",
            "Choose a payment method",
            "Confirm the payment",
            "Check the receipt"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "text-6",
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Illustrative example. Not a client artefact."
        }
      ],
      "notes": "Source: Australian_Financial_Services_APIOps_Cycles_Case_Study (1).docx, supplied by the user. Client anonymised. Engagement by APIsFirst with partner Osaango. Benefits are qualitative; no quantified delivery or business impact is claimed.\n\nNewly authored fictional example for this case study: a small business owner pays a supplier invoice through a partner portal. The supplied real example set informed only the method of connecting customer tasks, pains, gains and API capabilities. No source screenshot, wording, institution, system identifier, endpoint, number or product name has been reused. Unclear status and duplicate-payment concern are illustrative hypotheses, not measured client findings. Template source: local apiops-cycles-method-data, customerJourneyCanvas. https://apiopscycles.com/resources/customer-journey-canvas/ . Template by APIOps Cycles and Osaango Ltd, CC-BY-SA 4.0. Importable example JSON: assets/illustrative_supplier_payment_customerJourneyCanvas_en.json."
    },
    {
      "id": "value-proposition",
      "title": "API Value Proposition Canvas: the same journey",
      "elements": [
        {
          "id": "image-1",
          "type": "image",
          "asset": "filled-apiValuePropositionCanvas-neutral.png",
          "alt": "New illustrative filled API Value Proposition Canvas using the same supplier-payment tasks",
          "fit": "contain"
        },
        {
          "id": "text-2",
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Customer concern to API feature"
        },
        {
          "id": "text-3",
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Unclear payment status:\nshow progress and a receipt.\n\nRepeat-payment concern:\nprevent duplicate submissions."
        },
        {
          "id": "text-4",
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Candidate API products"
        },
        {
          "id": "bullets-5",
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Payee Check API",
            "Payment Methods API",
            "Payments API",
            "Payment Status API"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "text-6",
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Illustrative example. API names are provisional."
        }
      ],
      "notes": "Source: Australian_Financial_Services_APIOps_Cycles_Case_Study (1).docx, supplied by the user. Client anonymised. Engagement by APIsFirst with partner Osaango. Benefits are qualitative; no quantified delivery or business impact is claimed.\n\nThe fictional example continues exactly the same five customer tasks as the preceding Customer Journey Canvas. Proposed gain-enabling features: status visibility, a receipt ID and notification of the final result. Proposed pain-relieving features: checking payee details, preventing repeat submissions and explaining failed payments. The four API product names are illustrative candidates, not actual client systems, delivered APIs or verified product boundaries. All canvas notes were authored afresh. No reference screenshots or client-specific details are embedded. Template source: local apiops-cycles-method-data, apiValuePropositionCanvas. https://apiopscycles.com/resources/api-value-proposition-canvas/ . Template by APIOps Cycles, Alexander Osterwalder and Osaango Ltd, CC-BY-SA 4.0. Importable example JSON: assets/illustrative_supplier_payment_apiValuePropositionCanvas_en.json."
    },
    {
      "id": "architecture-inputs",
      "title": "Architecture: business needs become operating decisions",
      "elements": [
        {
          "id": "table-1",
          "type": "table",
          "rows": [
            [
              "Method input",
              "Decision it supports",
              "Review with"
            ],
            [
              "Business Impact Canvas",
              "Failure consequences, resilience\nand acceptable degradation",
              "Product owner, security\nand operations"
            ],
            [
              "Locations Canvas",
              "Trust boundaries, connectivity\nand data-location constraints",
              "Architecture, security\nand compliance"
            ],
            [
              "Capacity Canvas",
              "Demand, peaks, availability\nand scaling assumptions",
              "Business and platform\nowners"
            ],
            [
              "Metrics and analytics",
              "Observable service behaviour\nand consumer outcomes",
              "Operations and API\nproduct owner"
            ]
          ],
          "header": true,
          "emphasizeFirstColumn": true
        },
        {
          "id": "text-2",
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The next station needs architecture choices that stakeholders have reviewed."
        }
      ],
      "notes": "APIOps introduction. Technical continuation is illustrative; no client implementation or measured improvement is claimed.\n\nCanonical API Platform Architecture resources and the API Design entry criteria require validated architecture and shared conventions. Canvas contents inform decisions; they do not prescribe a vendor or deployment topology.\n\nSources checked 3 November 2026:\nmethod-engine: buildStationResourceData(api-platform-architecture), buildStartData(en, api-productization-cycle)"
    },
    {
      "id": "ai-build",
      "title": "Building with AI works best as a bounded change",
      "elements": [
        {
          "id": "numbered-1",
          "type": "numbered",
          "items": [
            "Select one reviewed operation and its acceptance criteria.",
            "Ask for an implementation diff and tests against the contract.",
            "Run checks, inspect behaviour and record unresolved decisions.",
            "Have the responsible people review the evidence before merging."
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "image-2",
          "type": "image",
          "asset": "intro-ai-build.png",
          "alt": "Generated conceptual APIOps scene of AI-assisted implementation and human review",
          "fit": "contain"
        },
        {
          "id": "text-3",
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Suggested task: implement payment submission with replay and cross-partner denial tests."
        }
      ],
      "notes": "APIOps introduction. Technical continuation is illustrative; no client implementation or measured improvement is claimed.\n\nRecommended iterative working pattern. The validated contract, domain rules, architecture decisions and critical journeys constrain the implementation. The example task is illustrative. AI output requires review and execution of meaningful tests. Source guidance explicitly connects delivery with prior method outputs. Visual generated with the built-in image generation tool using the APIOps scarf-human style; conceptual, not a client workshop photograph.\n\nSources checked 3 November 2026:\nsrc/snippets/api-design-principles-guidance.md\npackages/create-apiops/template/AGENTS.md"
    },
    {
      "id": "first-cycle",
      "title": "A first working cycle",
      "elements": [
        {
          "id": "numbered-1",
          "type": "numbered",
          "items": [
            "Agree one API product, a consumer journey and an accountable owner.",
            "Record the architecture choices and review one contract.",
            "Build one path with AI assistance and meaningful tests.",
            "Lint, audit and share findings before the next release decision."
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "text-2",
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Method and project resources"
        },
        {
          "id": "bullets-3",
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "apiopscycles.com",
            "apiops-cycles-method-data",
            "create-apiops",
            "CanvasCreator"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "text-4",
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Local reference versions checked: method-data 4.2.1 and create-apiops 1.1.4. 3 November 2026."
        }
      ],
      "notes": "APIOps introduction. Technical continuation is illustrative; no client implementation or measured improvement is claimed.\n\nSuggested introduction-to-practice sequence. Sources reviewed include the supplied anonymised Word case study, the previously prepared strategy case deck, installed apiops-cycles-method-data 4.2.1, create-apiops 1.1.4 template and initializer, and the matching local method-data repository. Technical examples and AI workflow recommendations are newly authored illustrative continuations. The case benefits remain qualitative. The local scaffold was generated with --no-install, so no live payment service, Spectral run or runtime test outcome is claimed.\n\nSources checked 3 November 2026:\nhttps://apiopscycles.com/\nhttps://github.com/APIOpsCycles/apiops-cycles-method-data\npackages/create-apiops/README.md\nsrc/lib/method-engine.js"
    }
